    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "Your situation is understandable and frustrating. Having stronger formal qualifications on paper (a Master’s in urban planning, licensure, and 12 years of municipal experience) while losing out to a candidate with broader/different qualifications before an all-male panel naturally raises questions about equity and process.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what this composition may signify and how you might consider handling it.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Is an all-male panel a \"sign of anything\"?\n\n* **A Failure of Modern HR Best Practice:** In public administration, standard best practice for executive hiring is to assemble a diverse interview panel specifically to mitigate affinity bias, groupthink, and blind spots. An all-male panel for a director-level role suggests either that the municipality lacks modern diversity and inclusion recruitment policies, or that senior leadership simply comprises men who interviewed without considering panel composition.\n* **A High-Risk Environment for Affinity Bias:** An all-male panel does not prove intentional discrimination on its own, but it significantly elevates the risk of **unconscious affinity bias** (\"similar-to-me\" bias). Panelists often subconsciously gravitate toward candidates with whom they feel an easy personal rapport or whom they perceive as \"a good cultural fit.\"\n* **Subjective vs. Objective Criteria:** In director-level roles, municipalities frequently weigh \"soft\" leadership qualities (political acumen, council relations, conflict resolution, public consultation) over technical planning credentials. While public consultation experience is genuinely critical for planning directors dealing with controversial developments, relying heavily on subjective assessments can also serve as a convenient cover when selecting a favored candidate over a technically superior one.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Two-Week Silence on Written Feedback\n\nTwo weeks without a response to a formal request for feedback is common in local government:\n* Municipal HR processes are notoriously slow.\n* The formal offer and background checks for the successful candidate may still be finalizing.\n* Municipal legal/HR teams often vet written feedback for executive candidates cautiously to avoid opening the door to appeals or grievances.\n\nA two-week lapse is more likely administrative inertia or institutional caution than an active snub.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should you \"make something\" of it?\n\nHow you proceed depends on what outcome you are aiming for:\n\n#### Option A: Seek Professional Feedback First (Recommended Step)\nBefore escalating, follow up professionally on your feedback request. \n* Send a polite, constructive follow-up to the HR lead or hiring manager:\n  > *\"I wanted to follow up on my request for feedback regarding the Director of Planning competition. As I continue my professional development, understanding how the panel evaluated my candidacy and where other experience was prioritized would be immensely helpful.\"*\n* Pay close attention to what they highlight. If their feedback is vague, contradictory to the job description, or heavily reliant on undefined \"fit,\" it gives you a clearer picture of what occurred.\n\n#### Option B: Request Public Records / Competition Files\nBecause this is a municipality, the hiring process is typically governed by public sector merit principles and freedom of information / sunshine laws:\n* Interview notes, scoring matrices, and candidate evaluation sheets are public records (with private candidate details redacted).\n* In many jurisdictions, candidates have a right to access their own interview scoring and evaluation notes.\n* If you suspect non-merit factors played a decisive role, you can often request the competition records through a formal Freedom of Information (FOI / FOIA / ATIP) request without needing an attorney.\n\n#### Option C: Formal Discrimination Complaint or Legal Counsel\nIf you are considering filing a formal complaint (e.g., with a state/provincial human rights tribunal, the EEOC, or an internal municipal ombudsman):\n* **The evidentiary bar is high:** The fact that the panel was all-male and hired a male candidate is circumstantial; it is usually insufficient on its own to substantiate a claim of unlawful gender discrimination without documentation showing a departure from objective criteria, irregular scoring, or biased statements.\n* **Reputational risk in a small field:** Urban planning—particularly at the municipal director level—is a small community. Making formal allegations without concrete documentation from competition records can carry personal career consequences across neighboring municipalities.\n* **Consult first:** If you decide to go this route, speak confidentially with an employment lawyer or a representative from your professional planning association/union before taking formal action.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThe all-male panel is a legitimate red flag regarding the municipality’s recruitment standards and increases the likelihood of unconscious bias. However, it is not actionable proof on its own. \n\nThe most effective next step is to **follow up firmly but professionally on your request for feedback**, evaluate the substance of their rationale, and consider requesting the anonymized interview scoring records to determine whether the decision was grounded in standard rubric evaluation.\n",
